{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The fix is in place and verified.\n\n**The problem:** `build_system()` in `bot.py` sent the coaching instructions and the volatile athlete context (date, profile, facts) as one block with a single cache marker. Prompt caching hashes by prefix, so every `save_fact` rewrote the block and invalidated the whole ~1000-token prompt — exactly the `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` / `cache_creation_input_tokens` ~1000 pattern visible in `logs/usage.jsonl` after every memory write.\n\n**The fix** (`bot.py:130-138`): the system prompt is now two blocks, each with its own `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` marker — block 1 is the byte-stable `INSTRUCTIONS` constant, block 2 is the volatile `athlete_context()` tail. A memory write now re-caches only the ~60-token tail; the instructions stay hot. Also updated the README paragraph that described the old single-marker behavior.\n\n**Verification, without calling the external API:** I ran the real `reply()` tool loop with an injected fake LLM that emits a `save_fact` in round 1 and text in round 2, and asserted on the recorded requests: both blocks carry markers, block 1 is byte-identical across the memory write, block 2 changes by exactly the one new fact line, tools stay constant within the loop, and all facts remain included (no per-message selection). All checks passed; `data/athletes.json` and `logs/usage.jsonl` were restored afterward, and the `__pycache__` test artifact removed.\n\n**`ANSWER.md`** documents the change, the verification, and which parts of the colleague's note applied: the two-marker fix, byte-stability above the system prompt, refusing per-message fact selection, and usage logging all applied (the latter two were already true here); the tool-filtering win and the note's Telegram/MCP specifics didn't transfer. One thing to watch after deploy: a round following a `save_fact` should show `cache_creation_input_tokens` near the tail size only — if it's back at full-prompt size, something in block 1 isn't byte-stable."}
